.TH "bc_pty_signal" "3" "2026\-02\-01" "bash\-server 0.1.0" "bash\-server library"
.SH SYNOPSIS
.IP
.EX
#include \(dqbashclient.h\(dq

int bc_pty_signal(bc_client_t *c, const char *name);
.EE
.SH DESCRIPTION
Sends the signal identified by \f[B]name\f[R] to the PTY child process
on the server.
The server resolves the signal name to a number and calls
\f[CR]kill(2)\f[R] on the child\(cqs process group.
.PP
This is the preferred mechanism for delivering signals to the PTY child,
rather than writing control characters (e.g., Ctrl\-C as
\f[CR]\(dq\(rsx03\(dq\f[R]) via \f[CR]bc_pty_write(3)\f[R], because it
bypasses the terminal line discipline and is reliable regardless of the
child\(cqs terminal mode (raw, cbreak, or cooked).
.PP
A PTY session must have been previously spawned with
\f[CR]bc_pty_spawn(3)\f[R].
If no PTY session is active, this function returns
\f[CR]BC_ERR_PROTOCOL\f[R].
.SH Wire protocol
Sends on \f[CR]CHAN_PTY\f[R] (channel 5):
.IP
.EX
{\(dqaction\(dq:\(dqsignal\(dq,\(dqname\(dq:\(dqSIGINT\(dq}
.EE
.PP
Receives acknowledgement:
.IP
.EX
{\(dqtype\(dq:\(dqsignal_sent\(dq,\(dqname\(dq:\(dqSIGINT\(dq,\(dqpid\(dq:12345}
.EE
